The profiler's compile directory, and when the compiler reads its cache variable --
trn1.2xlarge 2026-09-11.

What went wrong. memory_read and memory_write set NEURON_COMPILE_CACHE_URL with
os.environ.setdefault to a fixed /tmp/pantheon_ccwork and never unset it. Every
workload after memory_read in the same process then compiled into the directory
the profiler searches, and the directory outlived the process. After one full pass
it held 63 NEFFs; the next `--test memory_read --repeat 3` searched 16 candidates,
newest first, never reached its own graph and fell back to the analytic Score
(trn1-repeat-and-order.log). The full pass itself had found the graph at candidate
6 of 7, because it ran before its own later workloads had filled the directory.

1. Is the variable read per compile or once per process? (cache_env_probe.py:
   four graphs of shapes no cache has seen, the variable changed between them)

    graph 0, env unset             -> /var/tmp/neuron-compile-cache: 1 new NEFF
    graph 1, env -> /tmp/ccprobe_a -> /tmp/ccprobe_a
    graph 2, env -> /tmp/ccprobe_b -> /tmp/ccprobe_b: 1 new NEFF
    graph 3, env -> /tmp/ccprobe_a -> /tmp/ccprobe_a: 2 new NEFFs (graphs 1 and 3)

   Per compile. So a kernel can own the variable for exactly as long as it runs.

    find /tmp/pantheon_ccwork -name '*.neff' | wc -l   ->   63

2. The fix, run: each memory kernel compiles into /tmp/pantheon_ccwork/<kernel>,
   set only inside cores.compile_cache and removed after (an explicit setting is
   still left alone).

   Run on the trn1 that had the 63-NEFF shared directory, with fresh per-kernel ones
   (cache_checks.sh). "old shared top level" counts NEFFs in the old shared root only;
   its root count rises because the per-kernel roots sit at the same find depth.

